course: chemistry
lesson: 33
title: The air we breathe
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Wave your hand fast and you feel something push back: air. You cannot see it, but it is real stuff, a mix of gases that takes up space and has weight.
explain: Push an "empty" glass upside down into a bowl of water and the water will not fill it, because the glass is full of air. Old diving bells kept people dry under water the same way.
explain: Air is mostly nitrogen, which hardly reacts, with about a fifth oxygen that we breathe, and tiny amounts of other gases such as carbon dioxide, which plants take in and which traps heat like a blanket.
explain: Air can get dirty with smoke and fumes, and people have changed it: we put holes in the ozone layer and are adding carbon dioxide. Yet when the world agreed to act, the ozone layer began to heal.
fact: Air is a mixture of gases; it takes up space and has weight, even though you cannot see it.
fact: About 78 parts in 100 of air are nitrogen, about 21 are oxygen, and about 1 is other gases; carbon dioxide is only a tiny part.
fact: Nitrogen hardly reacts, so crisp packets are filled with it to keep the crisps fresh.
fact: Air presses on us from all sides, and the air and liquids inside our bodies press back out, so we do not feel its weight.
fact: High on a mountain the air is thinner, so each breath holds less oxygen; climbers on the highest mountains often carry oxygen tanks.
fact: Smog is a dirty haze of smoke and car fumes that builds up in still air; cleaner cars and fuels have made many cities' air clearer.
fact: The ozone layer, high in the sky, blocks much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet light; some old spray-can and fridge gases damaged it.
fact: Those gases were banned by a world agreement in 1987, and the ozone hole has been reported to be slowly healing.
fact: Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide to the air; carbon dioxide traps heat like a blanket, so even a small rise warms the planet.
fact: Plants take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen while they use sunlight to make sugar; tiny plants in the ocean are thought to make about half of the world's oxygen.
fact: A few houseplants do very little to clean the air of a room; opening a window does far more.
fact: Fridges and air conditioners use special gases that soak up heat when they evaporate and give it out when squeezed; old fridges are recycled carefully so these gases do not escape.
fact: Air is full of tiny specks of dust, pollen and skin flakes; you see them dancing in a sunbeam because they reflect light, and pollen makes people with hay fever sneeze.
exercise: Why do you feel something when you wave your hand fast? | Air is real stuff that pushes back | Your hand is making a tiny wind of its own | Your skin is tired from moving | Light is bumping against your hand
exercise: Why does water not fill an upside-down "empty" glass pushed into a bowl? | Air inside takes up the space | Glass pushes water away | Water is afraid of glass | Water cannot go upward at all
exercise: What gas makes up most of the air? | Nitrogen | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Helium
exercise: About how much of the air is oxygen? | About a fifth | About half | Nearly all of it | Almost none of it
exercise: Why are crisp packets filled with nitrogen? | Nitrogen hardly reacts, so crisps stay fresh | Nitrogen makes the crisps taste saltier and crunchier | Nitrogen makes the bag float | Nitrogen is the gas in crisps' flavour
exercise: Why do climbers on very high mountains carry oxygen tanks? | Each breath up there holds less oxygen | Mountain air is poisonous to breathe in | There is no air at all on mountains | The cold freezes oxygen in the air
exercise: What is smog? | A dirty haze of smoke and fumes in still air | A thick white fog rolling in from the sea | A cloud of pollen in spring | Steam rising from all the city's swimming pools
exercise: What does the ozone layer do? | Blocks much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet | Keeps the air from floating off into space | Makes the sky look blue | Holds up the clouds
exercise: What happened after the world banned ozone-damaging gases in 1987? | The ozone hole was reported to be slowly healing | The ozone layer disappeared completely within a year | Nothing at all changed | The ozone hole grew twice as fast
exercise: What does carbon dioxide do in the air? | Traps heat like a blanket | Makes the sky look so blue | Gives us most of our oxygen | Blocks the ultraviolet light
exercise: Where does extra carbon dioxide in the air come from? | Burning coal, oil and gas | Plants growing in big forests | Ice slowly melting at the poles | Rain falling into the open sea
exercise: What do plants do with carbon dioxide? | Take it in and give out oxygen | Turn it into smoke | Store it in their leaves as water | Breathe it out in the middle of the day
exercise: Where does about half the world's oxygen come from? | Tiny plants in the ocean | Houseplants on windowsills | Volcanoes deep under the sea | Factories that make it for us
exercise: Why are old fridges recycled carefully? | Their gases must not escape | They contain real gold inside | They could start to rust away | They are much too heavy for bins
exercise: Why do you see dust dancing in a sunbeam? | Tiny specks in the air reflect the light | The sunlight itself is made of tiny bits of dust | Dust only appears in sunshine | The sun blows dust into the room
exercise: Why can pollen in the air make some people sneeze? | They have hay fever | Pollen is a poisonous gas | Pollen tickles everybody's nose | Pollen is made of pepper
test: Ava: "the glass is empty, there's nothing in it". What's true? | It is full of air, which takes up space | She's right, there is nothing at all | It is full of nitrogen only | It is full of water vapour only
test: If crisps were packed with ordinary air instead of nitrogen, what might happen? | They might go stale faster | They would stay fresh longer | They would turn into popcorn | They would get heavier
test: "carbon dioxide's such a tiny bit of the air, it can't matter, innit". What's true? | Even a small change traps more heat | He's right, it changes nothing at all, ever | It is actually most of the air | It cools the planet down
test: Nan says she bought three houseplants "to clean all the air in the flat". What's true? | A few plants do little; opening a window does more | Three plants clean the air of a whole flat | Plants take oxygen out of the air | Plants only clean the air at night
test: A cartoon climber on Everest says there's no air up there at all. What's true? | There is air, but each breath has less oxygen | There is no air at all above the highest clouds | The air is made of ice up there | There is more oxygen high up
test: "all that air on top of me weighs loads? then why don't I get squashed lol" | Air presses from all sides, even from inside | Air weighs nothing at all | Air only presses on tall people | Your skin is too tough and thick to feel it at all
test: "ozone hole's done, we wrecked it forever". What's been reported? | It is slowly healing after the 1987 ban | It is growing faster than ever | It has disappeared completely | It was never real at all
test: Why is the air clearer in many cities now than fifty years ago? | Cleaner cars and fuels | More fog rolling in from the sea | Fewer trees in the parks | Stronger summer sunlight
test: Your hay-fever mate sneezes all spring. "it's the air", he moans. What's in the air? | Pollen from plants | Too much nitrogen | Carbon dioxide | Ozone from the sky
test: If all the tiny plants in the sea died, what would happen? | About half the new oxygen would stop being made | More oxygen would be made than before | Nothing, forests make all the oxygen | Only the fish would notice any change
test: In an old diving bell, people kept dry under water. How? | Air trapped inside kept the water out | The bell was filled with dry sponges | Magnets pushed the water away from it | The water was frozen solid all around it
exam: Identify the gas which constitutes the greater part of the atmosphere. | Nitrogen | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Hydrogen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the ozone layer? | It absorbs much of the harmful ultraviolet radiation from the Sun | It retains the oxygen within the atmosphere | It produces the blue colour of the sky | It supports the weight of the clouds
exam: It is given that the combustion of coal, oil and gas releases carbon dioxide. Select the consequence consistent with the established facts. | Additional heat is retained by the atmosphere | The atmosphere becomes cooler | The ozone layer is immediately restored | The oxygen content rises markedly
exam: Ascertain the reason for which mountaineers at very high altitude frequently carry oxygen. | Each breath contains less oxygen in the thinner air | The air at altitude is entirely absent | The air at altitude is poisonous | Oxygen freezes at high altitude
